Deprecated and experimental flags become compiler attributes on record fields
and on whole domains, not only on types, commands and events.

  $ cat > browser.json << 'EOF'
  > {"domains":[
  >   {"domain":"Demo","types":[
  >     {"id":"Thing","type":"object","properties":[
  >       {"name":"fresh","type":"string"},
  >       {"name":"old","type":"string","optional":true,"deprecated":true},
  >       {"name":"trial","type":"integer","experimental":true}
  >     ]}
  >   ]},
  >   {"domain":"Legacy","deprecated":true,"experimental":true,"types":[
  >     {"id":"Marker","type":"string"}
  >   ],"commands":[
  >     {"name":"poke"},
  >     {"name":"moved","redirect":"Modern"},
  >     {"name":"gone","deprecated":true,"redirect":"Modern"}
  >   ]}
  > ]}
  > EOF
  $ echo '{"domains":[]}' > js.json
  $ mkdir out && cdp-gen generate browser.json js.json out Demo,Legacy > /dev/null

A flagged field carries the attribute on the field itself.

  $ grep -A3 'thing = {' out/cdp_demo_types.ml
  type thing = {
    fresh : string [@key "fresh"];
    old : string option [@key "old"] [@option] [@json.drop_default] [@ocaml.deprecated "deprecated in CDP"];
    trial : int [@key "trial"] [@alert experimental "experimental in CDP, may change with Chrome"];

A flagged domain marks both of its files at the top and its index aliases.

  $ head -5 out/cdp_legacy_types.ml
  (* Generated by cdp-gen from Chrome DevTools Protocol unknown. DO NOT EDIT. *)
  
  [@@@ocaml.deprecated "deprecated in CDP"]
  [@@@alert experimental "experimental in CDP, may change with Chrome"]
  


  $ head -5 out/cdp_legacy.ml
  (* Generated by cdp-gen from Chrome DevTools Protocol unknown. DO NOT EDIT. *)
  
  [@@@ocaml.deprecated "deprecated in CDP"]
  [@@@alert experimental "experimental in CDP, may change with Chrome"]
  


  $ grep -A2 'module Legacy' out/cdp.ml
  module Legacy = Cdp_legacy
  [@@ocaml.deprecated "deprecated in CDP"]
  [@@alert experimental "experimental in CDP, may change with Chrome"]
  module Legacy_types = Cdp_legacy_types
  [@@ocaml.deprecated "deprecated in CDP"]
  [@@alert experimental "experimental in CDP, may change with Chrome"]

A redirected command names the domain that now handles it. Without a
deprecated flag it gets its own alert; with one, the target joins the message.

  $ grep -B1 'redirected' out/cdp_legacy.ml
  end
  [@@alert redirected "redirected to the Modern domain in CDP"]
  --
  end
  [@@ocaml.deprecated "deprecated in CDP, redirected to the Modern domain"]

An unflagged domain has none of this.

  $ head -3 out/cdp_demo.ml
  (* Generated by cdp-gen from Chrome DevTools Protocol unknown. DO NOT EDIT. *)
  
  include Cdp_demo_types

  $ grep -c 'alert\|deprecated' out/cdp_demo.ml
  0
  [1]
